WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.260
From cold-blooded murder to toxic&nbsp;
mind games, Yellowstone has it all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:08.040
but it's complicated. So grab&nbsp;
your cowboy hat and get ready,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:12.080
because we're taking a look at the&nbsp;
Yellowstone scenes that didn't age well.

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:16.140
In the Season 2 episode "Resurrection&nbsp;
Day," the level of violence we've become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:20.760
accustomed to on Yellowstone rises to a new&nbsp;
level. As the only daughter of the Duttons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:22.820
Beth is known for her bold personality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:27.480
which usually takes the form of fiery comebacks&nbsp;
and an abrasive attitude. It's almost a surprise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:31.620
then, when she's attacked at her office. As&nbsp;
Rip desperately tries to reach Beth before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:36.420
it's too late, two masked men sent by Malcolm&nbsp;
Beck storm the premises, hoping to scare her.

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:38.920
"All that tough talk, Beth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:45.480
I've got the cure for that. We'll see how&nbsp;
tough you are after I give it to you."

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:50.700
What's most notable about this scene is just how&nbsp;
violent it is. A fearful Beth is left bloodied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:55.560
and vulnerable in the grip of both men. That's&nbsp;
not to say she doesn't fight back. She tries to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:59.700
gouge their eyes out and throws them through&nbsp;
glass walls. The fight is a testament to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:05.180
fact that Yellowstone is willing to "go there" —&nbsp;
but also that it can take its grittiness too far.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:09.360
What's more, it doesn't send a great message&nbsp;
about violence against women. Although it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:14.340
commendable to show what can actually happen so&nbsp;
explicitly, it's also a straight-up brutal watch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:18.300
The most heartbreaking moment&nbsp;
comes when Beth lets herself cry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:23.480
the tough, gritty exterior she's spent a&nbsp;
lifetime building up temporarily broken.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.140
There are few people who inspire more fear&nbsp;
on the ranch than Lloyd Pierce. He might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:32.160
even outstrip the infamously brutal Rip&nbsp;
Wheeler in this department. In the Season&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:36.540
4 episode "Winning or Learning," the stage&nbsp;
is set for an incredibly questionable brawl,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:40.140
with Rip setting Lloyd against Walker.&nbsp;
While Lloyd maintains the upper hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:44.280
the pair go for round two a few episodes&nbsp;
later, with Lloyd beating Walker to a pulp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:48.540
in the middle of the ranch. To teach Lloyd&nbsp;
a lesson, Rip smashes his hand with a boot.

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:52.320
Though it's unsurprising to see a&nbsp;
Yellowstone brawl in such gory detail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:57.420
it doesn't age well within the big picture&nbsp;
of Rip's character development. His violent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:01.860
outburst is his own fault — something he quickly&nbsp;
seems to realize as he leaves Lloyd bleeding.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:05.160
"There's no fighting on this&nbsp;
ranch. You want to fight somebody,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:07.380
you come fight me. I'll fight you all f—in' day."

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:11.580
Beyond the brutality lies the question of&nbsp;
whether or not this lesson is necessary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:16.620
or if Rip overdid it. Even though the fight&nbsp;
sets Rip up for a juicy redemption arc,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:20.940
it does a disservice to the storylines&nbsp;
and relationship that come before it.

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:23.400
Though uncomfortable scenes&nbsp;
often contain great messages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:29.100
that doesn't make them any easier to watch. In&nbsp;
the Season 3 episode "I Killed A Man Today,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:33.267
Monica finds herself in trouble after&nbsp;
catching a ride with a known predator.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.267 --> 00:02:36.120
After trying to persuade her to leave&nbsp;
the vehicle to get better phone service,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:41.400
he becomes violent and forcefully restrains her on&nbsp;
the ground. But Monica has put herself in the line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:46.920
of fire in the name of a sting. A sniper swiftly&nbsp;
brings the predator to his arguably deserved end.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:50.640
While it's admirable for Yellowstone to take on&nbsp;
the issue of violence against Indigenous women,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:57.060
the scene has some questionable side effects.&nbsp;
Simply put, it's terrible to watch. And the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:00.600
scene reinforces the idea that women need&nbsp;
to relive their trauma for the sake of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:05.160
the greater good. Monica is visibly shaken&nbsp;
by what she's gone through. She's strong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:10.380
but even the most rock-solid person would be&nbsp;
rattled by such an encounter. Opening up emotional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:16.080
wounds is commonplace on Yellowstone, but it&nbsp;
could, perhaps, be handled more sensitively.

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:21.480
As far as the Yellowstone universe goes, Sheriff&nbsp;
Donnie Haskell wasn't a major player. Throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:25.680
the first four seasons, his questionable morals&nbsp;
and alliance with the Dutton family often fade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:29.400
into the background. That being said, his&nbsp;
sudden death takes viewers by surprise and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:34.680
leads to moments that are excruciating to watch.&nbsp;
In "No Kindness for the Coward," John and Rip get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:39.360
involved in a diner hold-up. Mid-robbery, John&nbsp;
storms in from the back kitchen to try and take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:43.860
out the thieves, and Donnie gets caught in the&nbsp;
crossfire, taking a fatal blow to the throat.

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:49.140
To make matters worse, Donnie tries to call his&nbsp;
daughter, only managing to get a few words out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:53.940
before he dies. John is left to tell Donnie's&nbsp;
daughter what's happened. The final moments of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:58.620
the scene are incredibly intense and especially&nbsp;
hard to watch for anyone who's suddenly lost a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:03.120
loved one. And afterward, audiences are left with&nbsp;
the knowledge that Donnie's death could have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:07.320
prevented if John and Rip hadn't gotten involved.&nbsp;
Is the scene just a case of someone playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:14.040
the hero for their own reasons? If so, that's&nbsp;
seriously — perhaps unnecessarily — wrenching.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:18.960
Like the Montagues and Capulets of Romeo&nbsp;
and Juliet, the ongoing war between the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:22.920
Duttons and Becks is neverending. It's&nbsp;
also a lynchpin of the Yellowstone story.

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:27.720
"I'm not gonna lose this one, Rip. Not this one."

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:32.400
By the time fans get to Season 2's "Sins of&nbsp;
the Father," the feud is well established.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:36.960
Many of the Becks have met a grisly end&nbsp;
at the hands of the Duttons, but no death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:42.480
is more unnecessary or confusing than Teal's&nbsp;
sudden demise. As Kayce sneaks into his house,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:47.100
he finds an unsuspecting Teal on the toilet&nbsp;
and shoots him. As Kayce watches him die,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:51.240
he tells Teal he promised his wife to kill&nbsp;
him in the most humiliating way possible.

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:55.500
As far as humiliation goes, the scene&nbsp;
hits the nail on the head. The whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:59.820
sequence is awkward and excruciating to&nbsp;
watch, not to mention fairly irrelevant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:03.480
to the bigger story. There's no doubt&nbsp;
Teal brought this misery on himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:08.160
but couldn't he die with dignity intact?&nbsp;
Somewhere between funny and hard-hitting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:13.420
the scene finds itself in an emotional no man's&nbsp;
land that leaves viewers confused and embarrassed.

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:18.240
Sibling rivalry is at the heart of any good&nbsp;
family drama, but the tension between Beth and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:23.460
Jamie is unique. The intensity of their hatred&nbsp;
is palpable from the very first episode, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:28.020
the reason behind it isn't revealed in Season 3's&nbsp;
"Cowboys and Dreamers." In a flashback sequence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:32.220
teenage Beth confides in Jamie that she&nbsp;
needs to hide a pregnancy. Jamie takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:35.880
her out of town for an abortion, but neglects&nbsp;
to mention that it will leave her infertile.

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:37.560
"You don't want to do this here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:43.800
A requirement for patients receiving an&nbsp;
abortion at this clinic is sterilization."

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:49.200
It goes without saying that exploring pregnancy&nbsp;
and abortion on TV is going to be contentious.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:56.160
Regardless, in the eyes of many viewers, this&nbsp;
scene takes things too far. Some fans find the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:00.660
storyline outright insulting, while some medical&nbsp;
professionals take issue with its inaccuracy.

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:06.000
It doesn't take long for Yellowstone to establish&nbsp;
exactly what kind of show it is. In the opening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:10.440
moments of the pilot, audiences are introduced to&nbsp;
John Dutton, currently in the middle of trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:15.060
protect his ranch from land developers. A stream&nbsp;
of blood runs down John's face as he tries to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:21.180
comfort a suffering horse, then puts it out of its&nbsp;
misery. We later learn that it's all a result of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:25.935
nasty traffic accident. There's something about&nbsp;
seeing an animal suffer that hits extra hard.

00:06:25.935 --> 00:06:28.755
"The things we lose to keep you fed."

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:35.040
Yellowstone's stars have had to clarify&nbsp;
that the show doesn't abuse animals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:39.300
but PETA has actively encouraged viewers&nbsp;
to boycott the show. For some viewers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:42.540
the opening scene is a questionable&nbsp;
introduction to the series as a whole.

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:46.500
When it comes to Rip's wrath&nbsp;
on Yellowstone, no one is safe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:51.240
Characters take their well-being into their own&nbsp;
hands when they decide to cross the Dutton family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:55.440
and Season 3's "Meaner Than Evil" shows&nbsp;
just how terrible the consequences can be.

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:00.420
"That son of a b—h has something&nbsp;
belongs to me and I want it back."

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:06.120
As a major antagonist across multiple seasons,&nbsp;
Wade Morrow quickly loses the respect of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:10.440
family — especially once he's accused of stealing.&nbsp;
After betraying the Duttons and their ranch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:14.940
Wade is tracked down by Rip and murdered in&nbsp;
the middle of nowhere. As a branded worker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:18.360
Wade also has his brand cut&nbsp;
off before his body is dumped.

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:22.320
Yellowstone routinely pushes the boundaries&nbsp;
for what can be shown on cable TV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:26.220
but this scene might just take the&nbsp;
biscuit. Viewers without the stomach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:30.060
for gore will definitely need to look&nbsp;
away, as close-ups of the brand removal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:34.700
paired with Wade's painful cries and the&nbsp;
knife sound effects, are a lot to take.

00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:38.000
"Mister, I don't know you, but&nbsp;
if you're wearing that brand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:42.840
you must be a bad man. These motherf—ers&nbsp;
want it back, you must be even worse."

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:48.480
Even though the series is set in the present day,&nbsp;
the violence feels uncomfortably old-fashioned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:53.760
bordering on barbaric. As arguably the&nbsp;
most agonizing death in Yellowstone so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:57.140
far, this scene is one of the&nbsp;
show's most shocking moments.

00:07:57.420 --> 00:08:02.100
In Season 2's "Blood the Boy," after Jamie tries&nbsp;
to recant his statement while cooperating with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:06.600
Sarah's campaign to expose corruption on the&nbsp;
Dutton ranch, the two find themselves alone.

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:11.520
"You're not the first source to have second&nbsp;
thoughts, but we're way past that point."

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:16.440
Things quickly get ugly. Sarah tells him that his&nbsp;
family deserves to lose everything, and Jamie is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:21.480
enraged. In a fury, he smashes Sarah's head into&nbsp;
the roof of their nearby car. As if that wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:26.760
bad enough, he finishes the job off by strangling&nbsp;
Sarah as she lies helplessly on the ground.

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:29.460
Although it goes without saying&nbsp;
that Sarah doesn't deserve to die,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:34.920
the way Jamie goes about killing her makes the&nbsp;
situation even worse. With a chilling stare,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.920 --> 00:08:39.300
Jamie leans into his dark side with no&nbsp;
hesitation. "I'm so sorry," he repeats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:43.140
as he strangles Sarah — but does that&nbsp;
matter? In fact, the dialogue makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:47.780
it even more uncomfortable to watch. No matter&nbsp;
what he's saying, Jamie commits a horrific act.

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:52.980
By the time viewers get to Season 3, there's just&nbsp;
one catastrophe missing from the Duttons' lives:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:58.020
a life-threatening explosion. The episode "The&nbsp;
World Is Purple" delivers that missing link,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:03.780
with Beth's assistant losing her life by opening&nbsp;
a package that also sets Beth's office ablaze. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.780 --> 00:09:07.920
hit on the Dutton family doesn't stop there.&nbsp;
Kayce narrowly escapes being gunned down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:12.840
while John is nearly the victim of a drive-by&nbsp;
shooting. It's incredible that any family members&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:17.220
survive the episode, let alone all of them.&nbsp;
But while the action might seem far-fetched,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:20.360
it's the gratuitous deaths that&nbsp;
call this scene into question.

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:24.580
In the case of Beth's office, it doesn't make&nbsp;
much sense for her assistant to be killed off.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:28.740
Not only does her loss seem completely&nbsp;
unnecessary, it also puts the spotlight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:33.180
back on the show's tendency to depict extreme&nbsp;
violence against women. If the creators wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:37.380
this episode to have maximum impact, surely&nbsp;
a Dutton needed to be lost along the way?

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:43.020
Context tells us everything, and when it comes&nbsp;
to the Dutton family, a lot needs to be unpacked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:46.980
in order to understand their dysfunctional&nbsp;
relationships. A hefty chunk of Yellowstone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:51.600
Season 5 is devoted to digging into the family's&nbsp;
history, with the episode "The Dream Is Not Me"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:56.100
focusing on John Dutton's rise to the top. While&nbsp;
John doesn't really change throughout the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.100 --> 00:10:00.840
we learn how his toxic personality has infected&nbsp;
Rip, who has never been able to lie to him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.840 --> 00:10:05.280
Teenage Rip was at odds with a boy called Rowdy&nbsp;
over Beth around the same time. He knocks Rowdy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:09.820
to the ground with a boulder — truly copying&nbsp;
the behavior he's grown up around all his life.

00:10:09.820 --> 00:10:13.800
In essence, this is a masterclass in&nbsp;
how to be a terrible person. Though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:16.020
Yellowstone features plenty&nbsp;
of rough-and-tumble guys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:20.534
toxic masculinity is likely not a&nbsp;
character choice that's going to age well.

00:10:20.534 --> 00:10:22.920
"I feel like I shouldn't be&nbsp;
watching but I can't stop."

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:24.100
"It's riveting."

00:10:24.100 --> 00:10:27.540
Not only do viewers see Rip forced to make a&nbsp;
choice that could have easily been avoided,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:31.200
but John is also complicit because he&nbsp;
gave Rip the brand that set him on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:35.400
path of no return. Though the relationship&nbsp;
between the pair is undoubtedly interesting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:38.280
Rip's choice to murder Rowdy, and the way he does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:42.920
it, is perhaps an unnecessary addition to the&nbsp;
problems that are already woven into the show.

